WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.954
Mrs. Medlock's story sounded like something in a book, and it did not make Mary feel happy.

2
00:00:09.955 --> 00:00:15.298
A house with a hundred rooms and nearly all of them shut up with their doors locked!

3
00:00:15.299 --> 00:00:19.161
A man with a crooked back who shut himself up too!

4
00:00:19.162 --> 00:00:26.671
Mary pressed her lips together and stared out the train window as it began to rain.

5
00:00:26.672 --> 00:00:33.032
"Don't expect to see Mr. Craven because you probably won't," said Mrs. Medlock.

6
00:00:33.033 --> 00:00:37.718
"And you must not expect that there will be people to talk to you.

7
00:00:37.719 --> 00:00:42.427
You'll have to play by yourself and look after yourself.

8
00:00:42.428 --> 00:00:48.541
You'll be told what rooms you can go into and what rooms you're to keep out of.

9
00:00:48.542 --> 00:00:50.536
There are many gardens.

10
00:00:50.537 --> 00:00:54.663
But when you're in the house, don't go wandering or poking about.

11
00:00:54.664 --> 00:00:57.606
Mr. Craven won't stand for that."

12
00:00:57.607 --> 00:01:02.723
"I won't want to go poking about," said Mary in a sour voice.

13
00:01:02.724 --> 00:01:09.606
She had felt sorry for Mr. Craven, but now she felt he deserved all the trouble that had come his way.

14
00:01:09.607 --> 00:01:12.787
He certainly sounded unpleasant.

15
00:01:12.788 --> 00:01:18.649
Mary turned away from Mrs. Medlock and watched the rain streaming down the window of the train.

16
00:01:18.650 --> 00:01:20.811
Soon she was asleep.

17
00:01:20.812 --> 00:01:24.227
When Mary woke up, it was quite dark.

18
00:01:24.228 --> 00:01:28.435
The train had stopped, and Mrs. Medlock was shaking her.

19
00:01:28.436 --> 00:01:29.946
"Open your eyes!

20
00:01:29.947 --> 00:01:34.676
We're at Thwaite Station, and we have a long drive ahead of us."

21
00:01:34.677 --> 00:01:39.223
Mary stood up and waited while Mrs. Medlock collected their things.

22
00:01:39.224 --> 00:01:43.285
They stepped down from the train and found a carriage waiting for them.

23
00:01:43.286 --> 00:01:45.707
A footman opened the door.

24
00:01:45.708 --> 00:01:52.642
After they were seated, he climbed up next to the driver, and they set off.

25
00:01:52.643 --> 00:01:57.283
Mary was not a shy child, and she was not exactly frightened.

26
00:01:57.284 --> 00:02:02.836
But there was no knowing what might happen in this strange house on the edge of the moor.

27
00:02:02.837 --> 00:02:07.748
"What is a moor?" she suddenly asked Mrs. Medlock.

28
00:02:07.749 --> 00:02:12.099
"If you look out the window in about ten minutes, you'll see one.

29
00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:17.132
You won't see much because it's a dark night, but you can see something.

30
00:02:17.133 --> 00:02:23.221
We have to drive eight kilometers across Missel Moor before we get to the manor."

31
00:02:23.222 --> 00:02:29.280
Mary asked no more questions but waited in the darkness, keeping her eyes on the window.

32
00:02:29.281 --> 00:02:36.570
They drove through a tiny village, where Mary saw a church, a few shops, and some whitewashed cottages.

33
00:02:36.571 --> 00:02:41.675
Soon they were on the highway, and she saw hedges and trees.

34
00:02:41.676 --> 00:02:45.607
After a while the horses began to go more slowly.

35
00:02:45.608 --> 00:02:48.433
There were no more hedges or trees.

36
00:02:48.434 --> 00:02:54.360
Mary could see nothing but a dense darkness beyond the yellow light of the carriage lamps.

37
00:02:54.361 --> 00:02:59.379
"We're on the moor now, sure enough," said Mrs. Medlock.

38
00:02:59.380 --> 00:03:04.176
The rough road seemed to cut through bushes and low-growing things.

39
00:03:04.177 --> 00:03:08.287
The wind began to make a wild, rushing sound.

40
00:03:08.288 --> 00:03:14.312
"It's . . . it's not the sea, is it?" asked Mary, looking at Mrs. Medlock.

41
00:03:14.313 --> 00:03:16.796
"It sounds just like the sea."

42
00:03:16.805 --> 00:03:21.611
"That's the wind blowing through the bushes," Mrs. Medlock said.

43
00:03:21.612 --> 00:03:29.941
"The moor is a dreary place, though there are plenty of people who like it, especially when the heather is in bloom.

44
00:03:29.942 --> 00:03:34.564
Nothing lives on it but wild ponies and sheep."

45
00:03:34.565 --> 00:03:38.059
On and on they drove through the darkness.

46
00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:42.957
The rain stopped, but the wind whistled and made strange sounds.

47
00:03:42.958 --> 00:03:48.041
Mary felt as if the drive—and the moor—would never end.

48
00:03:48.042 --> 00:03:51.695
"I don't like it," she said to herself.

49
00:03:51.696 --> 00:03:56.928
She pinched her lips tightly together for a moment. "I don't like it."

50
00:03:56.929 --> 00:04:00.122
The horses climbed a hilly part of the road.

51
00:04:00.123 --> 00:04:03.201
Just then Mary caught sight of a light.

52
00:04:03.202 --> 00:04:07.144
Mrs. Medlock saw it too and gave a sigh of relief.

53
00:04:07.145 --> 00:04:12.278
"I am so glad to see that bit of light twinkling!" she exclaimed.

54
00:04:12.279 --> 00:04:16.100
"We shall soon get a good cup of tea."

55
00:04:16.101 --> 00:04:24.863
But after the carriage passed through the gates of the estate, there were still three more kilometers to drive.

56
00:04:24.864 --> 00:04:28.546
At last they arrived at Misselthwaite Manor.

57
00:04:28.547 --> 00:04:33.021
When they stepped down from the carriage, they were standing in a courtyard.

58
00:04:33.022 --> 00:04:36.167
A servant opened the front door.

59
00:04:36.168 --> 00:04:41.030
As Mary walked into the enormous hall, she felt very small.

60
00:04:41.031 --> 00:04:44.464
A thin, old man stood near the servant.

61
00:04:44.465 --> 00:04:48.820
"You are to take her to her room," he said.

62
00:04:48.821 --> 00:04:54.619
"Mr. Craven doesn't want to see her. He's going to London in the morning."

63
00:04:54.620 --> 00:04:58.932
"Very well, Mr. Pitcher," said Mrs. Medlock.

64
00:04:58.933 --> 00:05:02.284
"Come along," she said to Mary.

65
00:05:02.285 --> 00:05:07.240
She led Mary up a broad staircase and down a long corridor.

66
00:05:07.241 --> 00:05:12.490
Then they went up a short flight of steps and through two more corridors.

67
00:05:12.491 --> 00:05:19.392
Finally they came to a room with a fire in the fireplace and supper on a table.

68
00:05:19.393 --> 00:05:23.368
"Well, here you are!" said Mrs. Medlock.

69
00:05:23.369 --> 00:05:31.338
"This room and the next are where you'll live—and you must not wander from them. Don't forget that!"

70
00:05:31.339 --> 00:05:42.504
Mary looked around. "I don't like it," she said to herself again.

